  [1mBut one decision can't wait, because it's load-bearing on the tasks app itself.[0m

  The fork: whether tasks are shared between people. That determines whether this app needs a server, accounts, and a
  database at all — or is a single-user page with local storage. It's the one choice here that is genuinely expensive to
  change later, since it decides the entire stack, and it also decides whether "notifications" means [3mother people did [0m
  [3msomething[0m or [3mtime passed[0m.

  - [1mMulti-user/shared[0m — buys you real notifications (assigned to you, someone commented, someone closed your task).
    Costs a backend, auth, and persistence from day one. Rules out staying a static page.
  - [1mSingle-user[0m — buys a working app in a fraction of the time, no infrastructure. Notifications shrink to time-based
    reminders (due soon, overdue) and there's no "they" in "tasks they care about." Rules out sharing without a later
    rewrite of the storage layer.
  - [1mSingle-user now, structured for multi-user later[0m — a middle path: local storage behind a data-access layer with task
    ownership modeled from the start. Costs some upfront indirection and it is not free to migrate — but it's far
    cheaper than retrofitting.

  My recommendation is the [1mmiddle path[0m only if you're unsure, and [1mmulti-user[0m if you already know other people will use
  this together. Your original sentence — "users get notified when tasks they care about change" — reads multi-user to
  me, since caring about someone else's change is the whole premise. But I'd rather you confirm that than have me assume
  a backend into existence.
